.Dd June 1, 2025
.Dt XO_FILTER 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xo_filter
.Nd output filtering for libxo\-based commands
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The filter feature allows applications to select which portions of
structured output are emitted, based on XPath-like expressions.
Only matching instances are written to the output stream;
non-matching instances are silently discarded.
.Pp
Filters are useful when an application emits a large dataset (for
example, all network sockets) but only a subset is of interest (for
example, established TCP connections).
The filter can be applied without modifying the application code.
Unlike traditional Unix tools, filters are aware of the fields and
their context.
.Sh EXPRESSION SYNTAX
Filters use an XPath-like expression language.
Expressions are used to select content hierarchies and specify
conditions for content to be selected.
.Pp
Expressions may include paths to elements, negated paths, wildcards,
predicate tests, literal strings, numbers, and function calls.
.Ss Paths
A single element name selects all elements with that name:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
chapter
.Ed
.Pp
Selects all elements named
.Li chapter .
.Pp
A slash-separated path selects child elements relative to their parents:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
doc/chapter/section/paragraph
.Ed
.Pp
Selects all
.Li paragraph
elements that are under a
.Li section ,
which must be under a
.Li chapter ,
which must be under a
.Li doc
element.
.Ss Path Negation
Prefixing a path expression with
.Li \&!
suppresses matching instances rather than emitting them.
When all filters use negation,
.Nm libxo
defaults to allowing output and suppresses only the matching instances.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\&!remote
.Ed
.Pp
Suppresses all elements named
.Li remote
and their descendants.
.Ss Wildcard Elements
An asterisk
.Pq Li *
matches any element name at that position in the path:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
one/*/three
.Ed
.Pp
Selects any
.Li three
element that is a direct child of an element with any name, which
must itself be a direct child of a
.Li one
element.
.Ss Absolute and Relative Paths
A path beginning with
.Li /
is anchored at the root of the output tree.
A path without a leading
.Li /
matches the named element at any depth in the output hierarchy.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
/one/two
one/two
.Ed
.Pp
The first expression matches
.Li two
elements that are children of a root-level
.Li one
element.
The second matches
.Li two
elements under any
.Li one
element in the tree, regardless of depth.
.Ss Predicate Tests
Square-bracket predicates filter elements by condition.
An expression in brackets is evaluated to true or false
(with boolean type conversion).
.Pp
Selecting by child element value:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
chapter[number == 1]
.Ed
.Pp
Selects all
.Li chapter
elements that contain a
.Li number
child element with value 1.
.Pp
Predicates can refer to attributes using a leading at-sign
.Pq Dq @  :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
chapter[@number == 1]
.Ed
.Pp
Predicates may perform arithmetic using addition
.Pq Li +  ,
subtraction
.Pq Li \-  ,
multiplication
.Pq Li *  ,
and division
.Pq Li div .
Comparisons allow type coercion when the operands have different types:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
item[cost * count >= 1500]
.Ed
.Pp
Selects any
.Li item
element where the product of its
.Li cost
and
.Li count
children is greater than or equal to 1500.
.Pp
Predicates may be applied to any step in a path:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
chapter[@number == 1]/section[@number == 2]
.Ed
.Pp
A numeric predicate selects the
.Em n Ns th
element from a set, using origin 1 (not 0):
.Bd -literal -offset indent
chapter[1]
.Ed
.Pp
Multiple predicates on the same step are ANDed; all must be true:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
chapter[@number > 15][page-count > 10]
.Ed
.Pp
Each step in a path may have zero or more predicates:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
one[a > 4]/two[b < 3]/three[c == 2]/four[d == 1]
.Ed
.Ss Literals
.Nm libxo
accepts string literals in single or double quotes,
in a way that will feel natural to C programmers.
Character escaping is supported:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
"test"
\&'\\tthat\\'s good\\n\\tnow what?\\n'
.Ed
.Ss Numbers
Integer, floating-point, and hexadecimal values are all supported:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
5, 10, 20000
3.14, 4.3e50, 0.125
0x20, 0xABCD, 0xfefefefe
.Ed
.Ss Function Calls
Functions may be used in expressions or predicate tests:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
chapter[number(section) > 15]
chapter[substring-before(title, "ne") == "O"]
.Ed
.Sh LIBXO EXTENSIONS
.Nm libxo
follows XPath syntax with the following extensions:
.Bl -tag -width "?:"
.It Li "&&"
May be used in place of the
.Dq and
operator.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
food[@fruit && @delicious]
.Ed
.It Li "||"
May be used in place of the
.Dq or
operator.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
fish[tropical || colorful]
.Ed
.It Li "=="
May be used in place of the
.Dq =
operator.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
tree[height == 10]
.Ed
.It Li \&!
May be used in place of the
.Fn not
function.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
socket[!contains(path, "/var/run/")]
.Ed
.It Li "_"
The underscore is the string concatenation operator,
equivalent to the
.Fn concat
function.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
thing[number("0x" _ hex-digits _ "00") > length]
.Ed
.It Li "?:"
Converted into
.Fn choose
and
.Fn choose2
function calls.
Both the
.Dq "condition ? if-true : if-false"
and
.Dq "condition ?: if-false"
styles are supported.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
item[(user ?: login ?: owner) == "phil"]
.Ed
.El
.Pp
The first four extensions
.Po Li && , Li || , Li == , Li \&!
.Pc
are provided so programmers do not learn habits that negatively affect
their ability to write in other languages, and to avoid surprising
users with familiar but differently-behaved syntax.
The last two
.Po Li _ , Li ?:
.Pc
are for convenience and readability;
the
.Dq ?:
operator is particularly useful and hard to live without.
.Pp
Strings may be concatenated with the underscore
.Pq Dq _
operator.
While this may seem an odd choice, many familiar operators such as
.Dq +
and
.Dq \&.
have other meanings in XPath and cannot be used.
.Sh ENABLING FILTERS
Filters can be enabled from the command line or from application code.
.Pp
From the command line, pass one or more
.Cm filter=
options to
.Fl \-libxo :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
netstat \-\-libxo xml,pretty,filter='socket[tcp-state=="ESTABLISHED"]'
.Ed
.Pp
Multiple filter expressions are combined as a union (logical OR):
an instance matches if it satisfies any one of the given expressions.
The same effect can be achieved with a single
.Dq |
separated expression or with repeated
.Cm filter=
options:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# Two expressions in a single option (union)
my-app \-\-libxo \
    filter='socket[tcp-state=="ESTABLISHED"]|interface[name=="eth0"]'

# Equivalently, two separate filter= options
my-app \-\-libxo filter='socket[tcp-state=="ESTABLISHED"]' \e
       \-\-libxo filter='interface[name=="eth0"]'
.Ed
.Pp
The
.Cm filter-warn
option enables diagnostic messages on standard error when filter
predicates reference fields that are not present in the output:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
my-app \-\-libxo filter=item[color=='red'],filter-warn
.Ed
.Pp
Application code can call
.Xr xo_add_filter 3
to add a filter expression to a handle:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
xo_add_filter(xop, "socket[tcp-state==\\"ESTABLISHED\\"]");
.Ed
.Sh FILTER EXPRESSION DETAIL
A filter path identifies which list to filter.
The path uses the same element names as the
.Fn xo_open_list
and
.Fn xo_open_instance
calls in the application.
.Pp
The filter system is an optional component of
.Nm libxo .
It is compiled and linked into the library only when the
.Cm \-\-enable-filters
option is passed to
.Xr configure 1 .
When filters are not compiled in, the
.Fn xo_add_filter
function is still present but returns an error.
.Pp
Filtering is provided via a dynamically loaded library, reducing the
overhead when filtering is not used.
If the filter library cannot be loaded, an error message is emitted.
.Pp
While content is being generated,
.Nm libxo
applies any active filters and decides what can be discarded
immediately, what can be emitted immediately, and what must be
buffered pending a final decision.
The quicker a decision can be made, the better the performance.
Filter expressions can be written to enable early decisions.
.Pp
For example, the expression
.Li /one/two/three
can discard any top-level element that is not
.Li one ,
then discard any child of
.Li one
that is not
.Li two ,
and so on.
.Pp
A predicate like
.Li "socket[type == \(dqtcp4\(dq]"
buffers any
.Li socket
element along with its complete hierarchy until a
.Li type
child element arrives.
At that point the predicate is evaluated and a decision to discard
or emit can be made.
.Ss Keys and Non-Key Fields
Key fields use the
.Dq k
modifier in the format string and must appear before non-key fields.
Since key fields appear first, buffering needs are reduced,
leading to better performance when predicates use keys.
Predicates using non-key fields require more rendering and buffering.
.Pp
If an instance closes while a predicate is still pending due to a
missing field, that predicate is evaluated with an empty value for
the missing field.
.Ss Sub-container Content
When an instance matches, all of its content is emitted, including
fields inside nested containers opened with
.Fn xo_open_container
inside the instance.
.Pp
However, the limitation on predicate paths means that subelements of
nested containers cannot be used in predicate tests.
.Pp
Content in nested containers that appears before the predicate field
is buffered along with the top-level fields.
If the predicate resolves to true, the nested container content is
committed as part of the matched instance.
If the predicate resolves to false, the entire instance, including
nested content, is discarded.
.Sh FUNCTIONS
The following XPath functions are supported in filter predicates.
.Ss String Functions
.Bl -column "substring-before(str, s)" "Returns part of str before the first s" -compact
.It Sy Function Ta Sy Description
.It Li "concat(s1, s2, ...)" Ta "Concatenates strings"
.It Li "contains(str, sub)" Ta "True when str contains sub as a substring"
.It Li "ends-with(str, suffix)" Ta "True when str ends with suffix"
.It Li "normalize-space(str)" Ta "Strips leading/trailing space; collapses runs"
.It Li "starts-with(str, prefix)" Ta "True when str begins with prefix"
.It Li "string-length(str)" Ta "Returns the length of str"
.It Li "substring(str, pos, len)" Ta "Returns a substring of str"
.It Li "substring-after(str, s)" Ta "Returns the part of str after the first s"
.It Li "substring-before(str, s)" Ta "Returns the part of str before the first s"
.It Li "translate(str, from, to)" Ta "Character-by-character translation"
.El
.Ss Numeric Functions
.Bl -column "ceiling(num)" "Rounds down to the nearest integer" -compact
.It Sy Function Ta Sy Description
.It Li "ceiling(num)" Ta "Rounds up to the nearest integer"
.It Li "floor(num)" Ta "Rounds down to the nearest integer"
.It Li "number(val)" Ta "Converts a value to a number"
.It Li "round(num)" Ta "Rounds to the nearest integer"
.It Li "sum(node-set)" Ta "Returns the sum of a node set"
.El
.Ss Boolean Functions
.Bl -column "boolean(val)" "Converts a value to a boolean" -compact
.It Sy Function Ta Sy Description
.It Li "boolean(val)" Ta "Converts a value to a boolean"
.It Li "false()" Ta "Always returns false"
.It Li "not(expr)" Ta "Negates the expression"
.It Li "true()" Ta "Always returns true"
.El
.Pp
Note that
.Li true()
and
.Li true
are different.
The former returns the boolean value true while the latter returns the
value of an element named
.Dq true ,
which is likely absent.
.Ss Regular Expression Functions
The
.Fn rematch
function matches a regular expression against a string.
It returns either a boolean indicating match success (the default)
or the portion of the string matching all or part of the regex.
It defaults to extended
.Pq ERE
regex matching.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
rematch(re, str, flags?)
.Ed
.Pp
The optional
.Fa flags
argument controls matching behavior:
.Bl -column "Flag" "Description 12345678901234567890123456789012" -compact
.It Sy Flag Ta Sy Description
.It Li b Ta "Use basic REs (BRE) instead of extended RE (ERE)"
.It Li i Ta "Case-insensitive matching (REG_ICASE)"
.It Li n Ta "Newline-sensitive matching (REG_NEWLINE)"
.It Li ^ Ta "Do not match at start of string (REG_NOTBOL)"
.It Li $ Ta "Do not match at end of string (REG_NOTEOL)"
.It Li s Ta "Return full match text as a string"
.It Li m Ta "Return first capture group as a string"
.It Li mN Ta "Return capture group N as a string (N: 0-9)"
.It Li p Ta "REG_POSIX (platform-specific; ignored if unavailable)"
.El
.Pp
Example: match sockets whose protocol version is 4 but not 6:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
socket[rematch("([a-z]+)([0-9]+)", protocol, "m2") == 4]
.Ed
.Pp
Further examples:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# Items whose label starts with "eth"
item[starts-with(label, "eth")]

# Items whose price rounds up to more than 3
item[ceiling(price) > 3]

# Sockets whose path does not contain "/var/run/"
socket[!contains(path, "/var/run/")]

# Items where the part of version after '-' equals "2"
item[substring-after(version, "-") == "2"]
.Ed
.Sh MULTIPLE PREDICATES
Multiple predicates on the same node are ANDed: all must be true for
the instance to be selected.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# Established TCP connections with pending received data
socket[tcp-state=="ESTABLISHED"][receive-bytes-waiting > 0]

# Interfaces that are ethernet and administratively up
interface[type=="ethernet"][state=="up"]
.Ed
.Sh SHELL QUOTING
Filter expressions contain characters that many shells interpret
specially, including
.Dq \&[ ,
.Dq \&] ,
.Dq \&" ,
.Dq \&' ,
.Dq = ,
and
.Dq | .
Quote the expression appropriately for the shell in use:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# bash / zsh
my-app \-\-libxo "filter=socket[tcp-state==\\"ESTABLISHED\\"]"

# tcsh
my-app \-\-libxo 'filter=socket[tcp-state=="ESTABLISHED"]'
.Ed
.Pp
Commas serve two purposes in
.Fl \-libxo
arguments: separating libxo options
.Pq e.g., Dq warn,pretty
and separating arguments in filter function calls
.Pq e.g., Dq starts-with(one, two) .
Option parsing happens first, so commas inside filter function
arguments must be escaped with a backslash:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
my-app \-\-libxo 'filter=food[substring(name\\, 1\\, 4) == "hush"]'
.Ed
.Sh UNSUPPORTED FEATURES
While filter expressions are based on XPath, many XPath features are
not supported, primarily due to complexity, buffering costs, and
performance impact on streaming data processing.
.Pp
The following XPath features are not supported:
.Bl -column "Nested predicates" "Multi-element paths inside predicates" -compact
.It Sy Feature Ta Sy Details
.It Li ".." Ta "Parent axis, e.g. item[../count == 4]"
.It Li "Node tests" Ta "comment, text, processing-instruction node tests"
.It Li "Identities" Ta "id and key functions"
.It Li "//" Ta "Descendant, e.g. top//deep or //deep"
.It Li "Variables" Ta "XPath variables, e.g. $one"
.It Li "Axis names" Ta "Any named axis"
.It Li "Nested predicates" Ta "Predicates within predicates, e.g. one[two[three]]"
.It Li "Predicate paths" Ta "Multi-element paths inside predicates, e.g. one[two/three]"
.El
.Pp
While no axis names are supported,
.Nm libxo
does support the
.Dq @
alias for the attribute axis.
.Pp
When an unsupported feature is parsed,
.Nm libxo
reports an error:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
% my-app \-\-libxo:XP,filter-warn,filter='item[..//node()]'
my-app: filter expression feature is unsupported: parent axis ('..')
my-app: filter expression feature is unsupported: descendant child (e.g. 'one//two')
my-app: filter expression feature is unsupported: 'node()'
my-app: could not add the requested filter
.Ed
.Sh FLAGS
.Bl -tag -width "XOF_FILTER_WARN"
.It Dv XOF_FILTER_WARN
Enable diagnostic output to standard error when runtime issues are
encountered while processing filters against incoming data.
The volume of output depends on the filter expressions and input data,
but can be useful when debugging filter expression behavior.
It corresponds to the
.Cm filter-warn
command-line option.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
my-app \-\-libxo filter='socket[number(tcp-state)==2]',filter-warn
my-app: invalid number value: 'tcp-state'
.Ed
.Pp
This message is repeated on each conversion error.
.El
.Sh THE "fdr" ENCODER
Filters are complex expressions, and the software that implements them
is also complex.
Despite the efforts of the author, it is possible
that expressions could be used that were not considered, tested, or
implemented.
In many cases, the setup required to support the failing
situation may not be something that can be shared to allow debugging.
To allow such debugging, an encoder named "fdr" (after the aviation
industry's "flight data recorder") allows the recording of the specific
output tags being passed to the filter software, making reproducing
problems trivial.
In addition, these failing cases can be added to
the test suite to ensure the quality of future releases.
.Pp
To use the FDR, just add "\-\-libxo @fdr" to your command line and
redirect output to a file suitable for submission.
As normal, all
"\-\-libxo" options must appear before any other options or arguments.
.Pp
Please be extremely careful to ensure no secret or sensitive data
appears in this output before submitting it as a bug attachment.
